The [Atomizer](/docs/kb/split-e2e-tasks) splits an end-to-end (e2e) test suite into one target per
test file, such as `e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts`.
The hash of an atomized task can cover the whole app in two ways:

- The task's `^production` input covers the projects the e2e project depends on.
  When the e2e project depends on the app, those include the app and every project the app depends
  on.
  Without a `production` named input, the input is `^default`.
- When Nx starts the app's server for the tests, each task depends on the server task.
  The server task is a continuous task, one that keeps running while the tests run.
  Nx adds the [inputs of continuous dependencies](/docs/reference/inputs#inputs-of-continuous-dependencies)
  to the hash of the tasks that depend on them.

A change to any feature therefore reruns every test file:

![Without scoping, checkout.spec.ts and search.spec.ts both hash checkout/, search/, cart/, and shared/, so an edit in search/ reruns both tests.](../../../assets/guides/running-tasks/e2e-hash-whole-app.svg)

## Choose how to scope a test file

Scope a test file's task by the projects it exercises or by the app code it imports.
Scoping by imports uses [Ultracache](/docs/reference/project-configuration#ultracache), which hashes
a task from the files it read on a recorded run instead of its declared file inputs.

| Scope          | By projects                                         | By imports                                                          |
| -------------- | --------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Directive      | `// @nx-depends-on: <projects>`                     | `// @nx-ignore-server-inputs`                                       |
| The hash keeps | The listed projects and the projects they depend on | The app code the test file imports, directly or through its imports |
| Requires       | None                                                | Ultracache in CI, with Nx Cloud                                     |
| Upkeep         | List each project the test exercises                | Import the app code the test exercises                              |

Scope by imports when the test's results depend only on code the test file can import, and by
projects otherwise.
To check which way fits a test, see [decide whether a test fits](#decide-whether-a-test-fits).

Each directive is a comment at the top of the test file.
The Playwright, Cypress, or Jest plugin reads it and changes the inputs or the server dependency it
infers for the file's atomized target.
The directive stays with the test file when you move or rename it.

You can also write that configuration in `project.json`, under the name of the atomized target.
The `inputs` and `dependsOn` you set there replace the arrays the plugin infers, so copy every
inferred entry you keep.
When you scope a test file in `project.json` instead of with a directive, don't add the
[spread token](/docs/reference/nx-json#spread-token), `"..."`, to these arrays.
The token restores `^production` and the server task's inputs.
The target name contains the test file's path, so the entry stops applying when you move or rename
the file.
Each new test file needs its own entry.

## Supported runners and setups

A directive changes only the atomized target of a test file.
The targets that run the whole suite or group the atomized targets, such as `e2e` and `e2e-ci`,
don't change.
So `nx affected -t e2e-ci` still selects the `e2e-ci` task by its unscoped inputs.
When it selects the task, it runs the task of every test file, because the `e2e-ci` task depends on
all of them.
Nx replays the cached result of each task whose hash didn't change.

| Runner and setup                                                                                                                            | `@nx-depends-on`                                          | `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`          |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| Playwright, with a `webServer` Nx runs as a server task                                                                                     | Scopes the inputs and leaves out the server task's inputs | Leaves out the server task's inputs |
| Playwright, with a `webServer` Playwright starts                                                                                            | Scopes the inputs                                         | No effect                           |
| Cypress, with a `ciWebServerCommand` that runs an Nx task                                                                                   | Scopes the inputs and leaves out the server task's inputs | Leaves out the server task's inputs |
| Cypress, with a `ciWebServerCommand` that doesn't run an Nx task, or with the preset's `webServerConfig.reuseExistingServer` set to `false` | Scopes the inputs                                         | No effect                           |
| Jest                                                                                                                                        | Scopes the inputs                                         | Ignored with a warning              |
| Vitest and Cypress component tests                                                                                                          | Not read                                                  | Not read                            |

No effect means the task has no server task, so there are no server inputs to leave out.

Leaving out the server task's inputs also drops any environment variables, runtime inputs, or
external dependencies that the server task declares.
To keep the ones the test needs, add them to the test file's target in `project.json`.
Add `"..."` to its `inputs` to keep the inputs the directive inferred.

Nx runs a Playwright `webServer` entry as a server task when the entry meets all of these
conditions:

- `reuseExistingServer` is `true`.
- It sets no `wait` pattern and no `env` variables.
- Its `command` is `nx run <project>:<target>` or `nx <target> <project>`, with no other arguments.
  A prefix such as `npx` or `pnpm exec` is fine.

Nx evaluates the Playwright configuration with its own environment variables.
In CI, `reuseExistingServer: !process.env.CI` is then `false`, so Playwright starts the server.
For the commands Nx recognizes and the `webServer` entries it leaves to Playwright, see
[Playwright web server readiness](/docs/kb/playwright-web-server-readiness).

The Cypress plugin creates atomized e2e tasks only when the `nxE2EPreset` options set
`ciWebServerCommand`.

## Scope a test file to its projects

To keep changes to other projects from rerunning a test file, list the projects it exercises in an
`@nx-depends-on` directive:

```ts
// apps/shop-e2e/src/e2e/checkout.cy.ts
// @nx-depends-on: checkout, checkout-utils
import { getSubmitButton } from '../support/app.po';
```

The plugin then infers this configuration for the `e2e-ci--src/e2e/checkout.cy.ts` target, shown
as a `project.json` entry:

```jsonc
// apps/shop-e2e/project.json
{
  "targets": {
    "e2e-ci--src/e2e/checkout.cy.ts": {
      "inputs": [
        "default",
        {
          "input": "production",
          "projects": ["checkout", "checkout-utils"],
          "always": true,
        },
        {
          "input": "production",
          "projects": ["checkout", "checkout-utils"],
          "dependencies": true,
          "always": true,
        },
        { "externalDependencies": ["cypress"] },
      ],
      "dependsOn": [
        {
          "target": "serve-static",
          "projects": ["shop"],
          "waitFor": "ready",
          "inputs": false,
        },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```

The directive changes the inferred configuration in these ways:

- The plugin replaces the dependency inputs it infers, the ones that start with `^`, with two
  inputs.
  In this Cypress example, the replaced input is `^production`.
  For Playwright, the replaced inputs are `^production` and `^{projectRoot}/tsconfig*.json`.
- Both new inputs use the `production` [named input](/docs/kb/configure-inputs), or `default` when
  there's no `production` named input.
  The first selects the listed projects.
  The second, with `"dependencies": true`, selects the projects they depend on.
- With `"always": true`, Ultracache keeps the files of these projects in the hash.
  The test runner doesn't read the code the server serves, so Ultracache would otherwise drop it.
- The dependency on the server task sets `"inputs": false`, so the hash leaves out the server
  task's inputs.
  The dependency keeps `"waitFor": "ready"`, which the plugin infers unless its
  `waitForServerReady` option is `false`.
  Without a server task, such as for Jest, only the inputs change.

Without Ultracache, the task's hash then covers the listed projects, the projects they depend on,
and the task's other inputs, such as `default`.
Under Ultracache, the `always` inputs stay in the hash next to the files the task read.
A change to a file that the hash doesn't cover leaves the hash unchanged, so Nx replays the cached
result.
Listing the app puts the whole app in the hash, because the second input selects every project the
app depends on.

{% aside type="caution" title="List every project the test exercises" %}
A change to a project left off the list reruns the test only when another input covers it.
The `"dependencies": true` input covers any project that a listed project depends on.
{% /aside %}

## Scope a test file to its imports

An e2e test drives the app through a browser, so the test runner doesn't read the app's code.
To hash the test from the app code it exercises, import that code into the test file.
The test runner reads those files when it loads the test file, and the recorded run lists them.
The `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` directive leaves the server task's inputs out of the task's hash, so
the hash covers the app code that the test file's imports reach:

![With @nx-ignore-server-inputs and imports of the app code, checkout.spec.ts hashes checkout/ and shared/, and search.spec.ts hashes search/ and shared/, so an edit in search/ reruns only search.spec.ts.](../../../assets/guides/running-tasks/e2e-hash-imports.svg)

{% aside type="note" title="Ultracache runs in CI with Nx Cloud" %}
Nx applies a recorded run only in CI, when your workspace has Ultracache turned on in Nx Cloud.
Nx Cloud must also have a recorded run for the task.
To stop Nx from applying a recorded run, set `NX_CLOUD_USE_ULTRACACHE` to `false`.
When Nx applies no recorded run, such as on your machine, the task hashes its declared inputs
without the server task's inputs.
An app change then reruns the test only when those inputs cover it.
For example, `^production` covers the app when the e2e project depends on the app.
{% /aside %}

Add `// @nx-ignore-server-inputs` to the top of the test file, and import the app code the test
exercises:

```ts
// apps/shop-e2e/src/checkout.spec.ts
// @nx-ignore-server-inputs
// This test's task hashes the app code imported below.
// Import the code the test exercises: https://nx.dev/docs/kb/scope-e2e-tests
import '../../shop/src/app/checkout/checkout-page';
import { expect, test } from '@playwright/test';

test('shows the order summary', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/checkout');
  await expect(
    page.getByRole('heading', { name: 'Order summary' })
  ).toBeVisible();
});
```

The two comment lines after the directive are optional.
They tell maintainers why the test imports modules it doesn't use.

The plugin then infers this configuration for the `e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts` target, shown as a
`project.json` entry:

```jsonc
// apps/shop-e2e/project.json
{
  "targets": {
    "e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts": {
      "dependsOn": [
        {
          "projects": ["shop"],
          "target": "serve",
          "waitFor": "ready",
          "inputs": false,
        },
        { "target": "e2e--wait-for-webserver" },
      ],
    },
  },
}
```

The second entry is the dependency on the inferred task that waits for the Playwright `webServer`
to be ready.
That task isn't continuous, so it adds no server inputs.
Its name can differ, and some setups have no such task.
When you write this configuration in `project.json`, copy the entry that `nx show project` lists.

### Files the recorded run covers

Ultracache stores the recorded run as file patterns, one for each folder the test runner read from
and each kind of file it read there.
Each pattern covers its folder, including subfolders.
JavaScript and TypeScript files count as one kind, and other files count by extension, such as
`.css`.
A new file of a pattern's kind in its folder or subfolders changes the hash, unless a pattern
excludes it.
A pattern can move up to a parent folder when the test runner read every existing file of that
kind under the parent.
A new file outside the folders the test runner read from changes the hash only when a pattern that
moved up covers it.

The patterns leave these files out of the hash:

- Existing files that the test runner didn't read.
- Files of the pattern's kind in an existing subfolder, when the subfolder has such files and the
  test runner read none of them.
  The exclusion also covers new files and new subfolders inside it.
- Files with a name ending such as `.page.ts`, when the test runner read none of them.
  This applies when two or more files under the pattern's folder have that ending.
  New files with the ending are left out too.

### Decide whether a test fits

A test fits when all the code it exercises is in modules the test file can import, such as pages,
components, and the services they call.
A test doesn't fit when its results depend on something the test runner doesn't read:

- Files the app loads by path instead of importing them, such as static assets and data it fetches
  at runtime.
  This includes templates and stylesheets that a component names by path, such as Angular
  `templateUrl` and `styleUrl` files.
- App code the test exercises but doesn't import, such as the app shell, layouts, and shared
  providers.
- Stylesheets the test runner doesn't read.
  Of the setups in [choose what to import](#choose-what-to-import), only Cypress with the Vite
  preprocessor reads the stylesheets that imports reach.
  Screenshot comparisons and visibility checks depend on stylesheets.
- Other services the app calls, such as an API server.
- The server task's configuration files, environment variables, runtime inputs, and external
  dependencies.

For a test that doesn't fit, scope it by projects.
To keep the server task's environment variables, runtime inputs, or external dependencies, see
[supported runners and setups](#supported-runners-and-setups).

{% aside type="caution" title="A missed change replays an old result" %}
A change to a file that the patterns leave out doesn't rerun the test.
Nx replays the earlier result, even when the change broke the feature.
An extra import that loads costs only extra reruns, so import more when you're unsure.
{% /aside %}

### Choose what to import

The test runner reads each module that the test file imports, and each module those modules
import.
Choose imports that reach the feature's code:

- Import the module of the feature the test exercises, such as its page or component.
  Avoid route tables, the app entry point, and barrel files that re-export many features.
  They make the task hash every feature they import.
- Import a lazy-loaded feature's module directly.
  Playwright doesn't read the modules that a dynamic `import()` loads, such as lazy-loaded routes.
  Cypress bundles them, so a route table makes the task hash every lazy-loaded route.
- Use a bare import, `import '<path>'`, unless the test uses an imported value.
  The test runner's TypeScript transform can remove an import whose names the file doesn't use, so
  the test runner never reads that module.

The test runner loads each imported module and runs its top-level code.
Playwright does this in Node.
Cypress bundles the test file with its preprocessor.
It uses the Vite preprocessor when the `nxE2EPreset` options set `bundler: 'vite'`, and its default
webpack preprocessor otherwise.
These setups handle the files that imports reach as follows:

| File that an import reaches                             | Playwright                                                 | Cypress with webpack | Cypress with Vite          |
| ------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | -------------------------- |
| A module that a dynamic `import()` loads                | Not read                                                   | Read                 | Read                       |
| A stylesheet                                            | Not read, or fails to load in an ECMAScript module package | Fails to bundle      | Read                       |
| A `.vue` file                                           | Fails to load                                              | Fails to bundle      | Fails without a Vue plugin |
| A file a component names by path, such as `templateUrl` | Not read                                                   | Not read             | Not read                   |

In Playwright, a module that fails to load fails the test file before its tests run.
The error points at the import.
A module whose top-level code uses browser globals such as `window` or `document` also fails to
load.
With a custom Cypress preprocessor, its configuration decides what it reads and what it can bundle.
For a feature that doesn't load, import the TypeScript modules it uses, or scope the test by
projects.

## Check the inferred configuration

Run `nx show project` for the e2e project:

```shell
nx show project shop-e2e --json
```

In the target of a scoped test file, the `dependsOn` entries for server tasks have
`"inputs": false`:

```json
{
  "targets": {
    "e2e-ci--src/checkout.spec.ts": {
      "dependsOn": [
        {
          "projects": ["shop"],
          "target": "serve",
          "waitFor": "ready",
          "inputs": false
        },
        { "target": "e2e--wait-for-webserver" }
      ]
    }
  }
}
```

With `@nx-depends-on`, the target's `inputs` also select the listed projects with `"always": true`.

A target default in `nx.json` that matches the atomized target replaces the inferred `inputs` and
`dependsOn`.
For example, a target default with a glob key that starts with `e2e-ci--` can match it.
To keep what the directive inferred, add `"..."` to each array the target default sets.
Set `"inputs": false` yourself on any server task dependency a target default adds.

## Lint and type-check the imports

The [`@nx/enforce-module-boundaries` rule](/docs/kb/enforce-module-boundaries#e2e-tests-that-hash-from-their-imports)
reports imports of app code from another project.
In a test file with `@nx-ignore-server-inputs`, the rule skips most of its checks on bare imports
of other projects.
It still checks imports that name values or types.
The rule looks for `@nx-ignore-server-inputs` in the file, so it runs every check on a test file
that has only `@nx-depends-on` or that you scope in `project.json`.

The `import/no-unassigned-import` rule of `eslint-plugin-import` and of the Oxlint import plugin
reports bare imports that its `allow` option doesn't match.
If you use it, disable it for the e2e project's test files instead of removing the imports.

When a test file imports `.tsx` files, set `jsx` in the `compilerOptions` of the e2e project's
TypeScript configuration.
For example, set `"jsx": "react-jsx"` so TypeScript can check the imported files.
With TypeScript project references, a missing `jsx` makes TypeScript report error TS6305 on the
import.
The message reads `Output file '<path>' has not been built from source file '<path>'`.
With path aliases, it reports error TS6142.

## Directive syntax

Both directives follow these rules:

- Put the directive in a line comment or a block comment before the first line of code.
  Blank lines, a `#!` line, and other comments can come before it.
  Nx stops reading at the first line of code, so a directive after it has no effect.
- Start the line with the directive, after `//` or a block comment's leading `*`.
  Nx ignores a directive in any other form, or with a misspelled name, without a warning.
- A test file can have both directives.
  Nx then scopes the file by projects, because `@nx-depends-on` also leaves out the server task's
  inputs.

`@nx-depends-on`:

- Put the colon right after its name, as in `// @nx-depends-on: checkout`.
- It takes project names or patterns, such as `tag:checkout`, separated by commas.
  Repeat it on more lines to list more projects.
- Nx ignores it with no value, such as `// @nx-depends-on:`, and logs a warning that names the test
  file.
- A listed name or pattern that matches no project is an error when Nx hashes the task.

`@nx-ignore-server-inputs`:

- It takes no value.
  Nx ignores it with one, such as `// @nx-ignore-server-inputs: false`, and logs a warning that
  names the test file.
